  "worked_solution": "We first write $44{u}^{8}$ as the product $4{u}^{8}\\cdot 11$ which contains a perfect square $(4{u}^{8}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{44{u}^{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4{u}^{8}\\cdot 11} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{u}^{8}\\cdot 11} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2{u}^{4})}^{2}\\cdot 11} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2{u}^{4})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(2{u}^{4})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & = \\\\ |2{u}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |{u}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & = \\\\ 2|u{|}^{4}\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
